================================================================================
Map tiles & geodata
================================================================================

KP Rück renders its incident map with MapLibre GL JS. Tiles come from one of two places
depending on the deployment's map mode (see docs/OFFLINE_MAPS.md):

- Online mode — OpenStreetMap raster tiles, fetched by the browser at runtime.
    © OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL. https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright
- Offline mode — vector tiles served by a self-hosted TileServer GL container, generated
    with planetiler (Apache-2.0) from Geofabrik OpenStreetMap extracts, and rendered in
    the browser from the tile server's own style document.
    Geofabrik extracts: https://download.geofabrik.de/ — the underlying data is
    © OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL.

No map tiles or OpenStreetMap extracts are redistributed in this repository; they are
fetched at runtime, or generated by the operator into a local volume. TileServer GL itself
is run as its own upstream container image and is likewise not redistributed here — see
https://github.com/maptiler/tileserver-gl for its terms.

Operators are responsible for honouring each provider's terms of use for their deployment,
including OpenStreetMap attribution in the map UI.

Per-station data — personnel, vehicles, materials, emergency plans and Reko photos — is not
part of this repository. It is seeded or entered into a deployment by the operator.

================================================================================
Key open-source libraries
================================================================================

Frontend: Next.js (MIT), React (MIT), Radix UI (MIT), MapLibre GL JS (BSD-3-Clause),
          react-map-gl (MIT), lucide-react (ISC), Pragmatic drag-and-drop (Apache-2.0),
          next-intl (MIT), Tailwind CSS (MIT), Zod (MIT), date-fns (MIT), cmdk (MIT).
Backend:  FastAPI (MIT), Starlette (BSD-3-Clause), SQLAlchemy (MIT), Pydantic (MIT),
          Alembic (MIT), uvicorn (BSD-3-Clause), PyJWT (MIT), bcrypt (Apache-2.0),
          Pillow (MIT-CMU), ReportLab (BSD), openpyxl (MIT), APScheduler (MIT),
          python-socketio (MIT), Markdown (BSD-3-Clause), httpx (BSD-3-Clause).

The print agent (tools/print-agent) has a deliberately dependency-free core, so a station
can run it on a bare Python install.

The complete, authoritative dependency lists with exact versions and licenses are in
frontend/package.json + frontend/pnpm-lock.yaml (frontend), backend/pyproject.toml +
backend/uv.lock (backend), and tools/print-agent/pyproject.toml (print agent). All direct
dependencies are AGPL-compatible.
